# Engineering Ultra‐Small Cerium‐Based Metal–Organic Frameworks Nanozymes for Efficient Antioxidative Treatment of Dry Eye Disease

## abstract
Dry eye disease (DED) is a widespread ophthalmic illness that inflicts massive economic and medical burdens on society. Although oxidative stress is closely associated with the onset of DED, barely any quantities of antioxidants are clinically available owing to low efficiency, toxicity, and poor bioavailability. Cerium‐based nanozymes possess promising enzyme‐mimetic activities in scavenging reactive oxygen species (ROS). Moreover, the ultra‐small metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have the potential for more efficient drug delivery, ROS elimination, and therapeutic efficacy. However, the development of ultra‐small cerium‐based MOFs (Ce‐MOFs) is limited to a small minority due to challenges in the synthetic process and stability. By simply adjusting raw ingredients concentrations, three types of Ce‐MOFs with distinct particle sizes are synthesized: Ce‐MOF 1 (213 nm), Ce‐MOF 2 (36 nm), and Ce‐MOF 3 (2 nm). In comparison, the obtained ultra‐small Ce‐MOF 3 exhibited superior ROS scavenging and antioxidant capacity, decreased cytotoxicity, and excellent ocular penetration. Therapeutically, Ce‐MOF 3 is demonstrated to be highly efficient in alleviating DED by suppressing oxidative stress and inflammation, boosting corneal epithelial repair, and facilitating tear secretion recovery. The novel ultra‐small Ce‐MOF 3 may serve as a promising alternative treatment for DED and other ROS‐related disorders when combined with ocular biocompatibility.

Dry eye disease (DED) is a widespread ophthalmic illness that inflicts massive economic and medical burdens on society. Although oxidative stress is closely associated with the onset of DED, barely any quantities of antioxidants are clinically available owing to low efficiency, toxicity, and poor bioavailability. Cerium-based nanozymes possess promising enzyme-mimetic activities in scavenging reactive oxygen species (ROS). Moreover, the ultra-small metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have the potential for more efficient drug delivery, ROS elimination, and therapeutic efficacy. However, the development of ultra-small cerium-based MOFs (Ce-MOFs) is limited to a small minority due to challenges in the synthetic process and stability. By simply adjusting raw ingredients concentrations, three types of Ce-MOFs with distinct particle sizes are synthesized: Ce-MOF 1 $(273\ \mathsf{n m})$ , Ce-MOF 2 $(36~\mathsf{n m})$ , and Ce-MOF 3 $(2\mathsf{n m})$ . In comparison, the obtained ultra-small Ce-MOF 3 exhibited superior ROS scavenging and antioxidant capacity, decreased cytotoxicity, and excellent ocular penetration. Therapeutically, Ce-MOF 3 is demonstrated to be highly efficient in alleviating DED by suppressing oxidative stress and inflammation, boosting corneal epithelial repair, and facilitating tear secretion recovery. The novel ultra-small Ce-MOF 3 may serve as a promising alternative treatment for DED and other ROS-related disorders when combined with ocular biocompatibility.
## 1. Introduction
Dry eye disease (DED), a prevalent multifactorial ophthalmic disease characterized by ocular discomfort and blurred vision, has an immense effect on patients’ daily lives.[1] With more prolonged and frequent exposure to digital gadgets, the incidence of DED is dramatically surging to as high as $5.28\%$ to $33.7\%$ globally, inflicting massive economic and medical burdens on society.[2] Overexpression of pro-inflammatory cytokines in ocular tissues contributes to the pathogenesis of DED by disrupting the biological function of meibomian and lacrimal glands, which leads to insufficient tear secretion and instability of lacrimal film.[3] Although current steroid-like and immunosuppressive medications can ease DED symptoms to some extent, pharmacological side effects and recurrence of symptoms render anti-DED therapy challenging.[4]
Oxidative stress injury denotes the disruption of cellular metabolism and destruction of cellular components caused by overproduction of reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as superoxide anions $(\mathsf{O}_{2}^{\bullet-})$ and hydrogen peroxide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\operatorname{O}_{2}\right)$ .[5] Located at the front of the eye, the cornea is directly exposed to ultraviolet light, radiation, and air pollution, as well as excessive application of mobile devices, all of which result in excessive production of ROS and cause oxidative stress injury to the ocular surface.[6] Previous studies have demonstrated that oxidative stress is closely associated with the occurrence of DED.[7] The increased lipid peroxidation products were detected in the tear film and conjunctiva of DED patients.[8] Thus, eliminating ROS with broad-spectrum antioxidants may be a viable approach for anti-DED treatment.
Antioxidative nanozymes are currently attracting significant attention due to their promising biomedicine application.[9] A unique redox cycle between the oxidation states of ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ is utilized by cerium-based nanozymes to specifically scavenge ROS and protect tissues from damage triggered by excessive ROS production.[10] The catalytic activity of cerium-based nanozymes for ROS depends on several factors, such as morphology, particle size, surface area, ion ratio of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{3+}$ to ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ on the surface, and so on.[11] Although several cerium-based nanozymes were reported to treat ocular surface diseases (e.g., DED), further clinical application would be limited on account of potential toxicity[12] and high dose required for therapeutic efficacy.[13]
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a category of porous hybrid materials formed by the coordination between metal ions and organic bridging ligands, and emerging MOFs-based nanozymes are characterized by adjustable and remarkable enzyme-mimicking properties.[14] Several studies revealed that cerium-based metal–organic frameworks (Ce-MOFs) performed promising and multifunctional properties in photocatalysis, biosensor, and catalytic hydrolysis.[15] It is worth noting that, nanoparticles with ultra-small particle size $(<10\mathrm{nm})$ have potential for more efficient drug delivery, ROS elimination, and therapeutic efficacy.[16] Moreover, the bioavailability of ocular drops is poor due to the presence of tightly packed cells in the ocular barrier, which restrict the penetration of nanoparticles with larger particle size.[17] However, only very few ultrasmall Ce-MOFs have been reported so far (Table S1, Supporting Information), which could be attributed to low efficiency and the laborious methods involved.[18] This motivates us to hypothesize that developing CeMOFs with ultra-small particle size would exhibit the incredible enzyme-mimicking activity of cerium-based nanomaterials, as well as potential antioxidant efficacy for anti-DED therapy.
In this study, Ce-MOF nanozymes with antioxidant activity were prepared through a hydrothermal method using ammonium cerium nitrate $((\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{6})$ and terephthalic acid $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)}$ as a core metal of Ce and organic ligand respectively. The three kinds of Ce-MOFs with different sizes were obtained, named Ce-MOF 1 $(213\mathrm{nm})$ , Ce-MOF 2 $\ensuremath{\left[36\mathrm{nm}\right]}$ , and Ce-MOF 3 $(2\ \mathrm{nm})$ in sequence as particle size decreased. Then their morphology, phase structure, enzyme-mimetic capacity, and other characterizations were studied in detail. Meanwhile, the CeMOFs exhibited an excellent inhibition of intracellular ROS levels in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -induced oxidative stress model in vitro, especially for Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes. Therapeutically, the Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes could efficiently penetrate ocular tissue and alleviate the DED, including suppressing oxidate stress injury, accelerating corneal epithelium repair, and facilitating the recovery of tear secretion. Integrating with the biocompatibility of CeMOF 3 both in vitro and in vivo, the novel Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes promise an efficient, reliable, and valuable strategy for anti-DED treatment.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Preparation and Characterization of Ce-MOFs Nanozymes
The previous strategies of preparing size-controllable inorganic nanocrystals lie in the initial nucleation, growth, and ripening, for example, high-temperature injection and liquid-solidsolution phase transfer.[19] In this study, the particle size of Ce-MOFs was simply controlled by the concentration of raw materials (Figure 1a). The variant concentration of raw materials in $32~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction volume was introduced into Teflon lined hydrothermal autoclave, followed by heating reaction at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ to obtain Ce-MOF 1 $(0.708\mathrm{~g~H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $2.32\ \mathrm{g\(NH_{4})_{2}C e(N O_{3})_{6}}$ , high concentration), Ce-MOF 2 $(0.354\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $1.16\ \mathrm{g}\ (\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{6}$ , middle concentration), and Ce-MOF 3 (0.177 g $\mathrm{~H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $0.558\mathrm{~g~}(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{6}$ , low concentration). The morphology of obtained Ce-MOFs was identified as nanoparticles with different features. The scanning electron microscope (SEM) imaging preliminarily showed that CeMOFs consisted of homogeneous nanoparticles of variant sizes, which decreased markedly in Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 as their input quality of raw materials was successively halved (Figure 1a, SEM). Consistent with SEM results, the transmission electron microscope (TEM) observation suggested that the size of Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 was approximately measured as 213, 36, and $2\:\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, indicating the evident impact of the raw materials concentration on the morphology of the final product (Figure 1a, TEM; Figure S1, Supporting Information).
Since the amount of raw materials affected the morphology of the product, the internal structure variation of Ce-MOFs was further studied. The three-dimensional crystal structure of CeMOFs was formed via coordination between a pair of carboxyl groups of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ and positive-charged cerium ions (Figure 2a). The phase structures of three kinds of Ce-MOFs nanoparticles with different sizes were first investigated through powder X-ray powder diffraction (XRD). It was found that the diffraction peaks of Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 nanoparticles were all the same, no other diffraction peaks appeared (Figure 2b). Meanwhile, the characteristic diffraction peaks of the three samples were the same as the phase structure of Ce-UIO-66-BDC,[20] indicating the successful synthesis of Ce-MOF. The decrease in the raw materials concentration had little effect on the phase structure, and the skeleton structure of Ce-MOF remained intact.
Moreover, the broadening of diffraction peaks was observed as the concentration of raw materials decreased, indicating the production of smaller particles. This finding was consistent with the particle size results presented in Figure 1a. The fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra of the Ce-MOFs were further characterized (Figure 2c). Similar characteristic peaks of functional group were observed in all three products, providing further confirmation that there was no variation in the functional groups. The characteristic peaks at 1641–1554, and $1435-$ $1373\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belonged to the stretching vibrations $\O_{\mathrm{assy}}(-\mathrm{COO-)}$ and $\operatorname{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sym}}(-\mathrm{COO-})$ of the carboxylate ions, while the peak at 500–700 $\dot{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ belonged to the $\mathrm{Ce-O}$ stretching vibration.[21] The strong OH stretching band at $3400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to physically adsorbed water molecules on the surface of the Ce-MOF crystals.
The valence state fluctuation of Ce-MOF 3 nanoparticles was further examined using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The survey scan spectra revealed the spin-orbit split of C, N, O, and Ce elements in the obtained Ce-MOF, indicating their predominant composition of these four elements (Figure 2d). The characteristic spin−orbit splits of Ce3d (Figure 2e), O1s (Figure 2f), N1s (Figure 2g), and C1s (Figure 2h) were depicted. Ce3d spectra was complicated due to the hybridization of the Ce4f with ligand orbitals and fractional occupancy, which was corresponding to the spin-orbit components assigned to $^3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $^3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ . Figure 2e displayed four peaks (labelled 1, 2, 4, and 5) corresponding to ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ ions and three additional peaks (labelled 3, 6, and 7) attributed to ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ ions. This observation suggested the coexistence of both ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ and ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ species within the generated nanoparticles. In Figure 2f, the O1s spectra exhibited a main peak (1) at $531.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ and three smaller peaks (2, 3, 4) located at 529, 531.4 and $533\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. The main peak (1) and the smaller peak (3) were attributed to surface chemisorbed oxygen, which contained $0^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{O}}^{2-}$ , ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}{}^{2-}$ , etc. The shoulder peak (2) was ascribed to the lattice oxygen in the obtained Ce-MOFs nanoparticles, while peak (4) was attributed to covalent oxygen species, such as surface $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The N1s spectra exhibited two weak peaks located at 400 and $407\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. The peak (1) was attributed to the N-H band, while the peak (2) located at $407\mathrm{eV}$ should be due to the ${\mathrm{NO_{3}}}^{-}$ groups (Figure 2g). The spectra of C1s spectra contained peaks corresponding to oxygen-containing groups between 285.5 and $289\mathrm{eV},$ , in addition to the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ -bonded carbon $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ at $284.5\mathrm{eV}.$ Typically, the C1s peaks at 285.6, 286.7, 287.7, and $289\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to the C─OH, C─O, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , and $_{\mathrm{HO-C=O}}$ groups, respectively (Figure 2h). Then, the thermal stability of the Ce-MOF 3 was characterized by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) in air. As shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information), the
TGA curve of Ce-MOF was consistent with the literature, and adsorbed water and DMF were lost in the temperature range of $150-$ $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , followed by framework decomposition at ${\approx}350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .[22] The DSC curve of the sample further confirmed the TGA result. The breakdown of the obtained Ce-MOF was speculated according to the endothermic peak at $358^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The Brunauer–Emmett– Teller (BET) surface area of Ce-MOF 3 was further calculated to be $145.78\ \mathrm{{m}^{2}\mathrm{{g}^{-1}}}$ in the relative pressure $(\mathrm{P}\ \mathrm{P_{o}}^{-1})$ ranging from 0.06 to 1 (Figure S3, Supporting Information). A slight increase of the adsorption isotherm was observed at high pressure region $(\mathrm{P~{P_{\circ}}^{-1}})=0.4\mathrm{-}1.0$ , which was attributed to the existence of interparticle porosity (mesoporosity, type IV isotherm) presented in the coordination polymeric framework.[23]
## 2.2. In Vitro ROS Scavenging Activity of Ce-MOFs Nanozymes
In pathogenesis of DED, the excessive production of ROS, such as $\mathsf{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , is a prominent inducer of cellular apoptosis and inflammation activation in ocular tissue. Specifically, corneal epithelial cells were directly involved in components of cornea by external stimuli, and increased ROS level was observed after benzalkonium chloride (BAC)-induced DED modeling (Figure S4, Supporting Information). Therefore, it is imperative to evaluate the ROS scavenging activity of Ce-MOFs nanozymes prior to commencing anti-DED therapy.
The Ce-MOFs were comprehensively investigated for their catalase (CAT) and superoxide dismutase (SOD) enzyme-like activity. The illustration depicted that Ce-MOFs nanozymes exhibited SOD-mimetic and CAT-mimetic activity, efficiently decomposing ${\sf O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure 3a). Firstly, the amount of remaining ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ was detected by hydrogen peroxide assay kit to determine the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ scavenging capacity of Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes. The results suggested that Ce-MOFs presented high-efficiency and concentration-dependent elimination of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure 3b). At a concentration of $640~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , all three types of Ce-MOFs exhibited scavenging rates exceeding $80\%$ , with Ce-MOF 3 demonstrating an exceptional rate of nearly $98\%$ . At low concentrations, Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 exhibited similar ability, while the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ scavenging ability of Ce-MOF 3 was superior to the other two from 160 to $640\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ with significance. It was observed that the Ce-MOFs solution underwent a color change to yellow (Figure 3b, insert image) and effervescence occurred immediately upon addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The higher concentration of Ce-MOFs generated the darker-color solution, possibly due to the surface valence changes of cerium ionic. Upon addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{3+}$ was oxidized into ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ , resulting in a gradual yellowing of the solution as the proportion of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{4+}$ increased. Consistent with the above result, the slope rate $K_{\mathrm{Ce-MOF}3}$ was also higher than the other two (Figure 3c). To investigate the impact of diverse storage condition $(-20,4$ , and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) on the catalytic properties of CeMOFs nanozymes, a stability experiment was conducted. At 12 weeks, varying degrees of reduced enzyme activity were observed ranging from $10\%$ to $37\%$ (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Compared with conditions of 4 and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , storaging at $-20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ might be a better selection for long-term preservation. The decline in catalytic activities of Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 showed no discernible disparity. After undergoing autoclaved sterilization ( $\mathrm{103\kPa}$ , $121^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 15 min), the property of Ce-MOF 1 and Ce-MOF 3 remained largely unchanged, except Ce-MOF 2 with a $15\%$ reduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ scavenging activity (Figure S7, Supporting Information).
In addition to CAT activity, SOD-mimetic Ce-MOFs nanozymes also exhibit potent $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ scavenging activity. The ${\sf O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ scavenging ability of Ce-MOF 3 was evaluated by electron spin resonance (ESR) assay. The peak of 5-tert-butoxycarbonyl-5- methyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (BMPO)/OOH• signal disappeared after the introduction of Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes, indicating their remarkable ability to scavenge $\mathsf{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ (Figure 3d). Next, exceptional SOD-mimetic activities of Ce-MOFs were observed in a dosedependent manner. Notably, Ce-MOF 3 demonstrated slightly superior properties to Ce-MOF 1 at $160~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Figure 3e). Moreover, more than half of $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ was decomposed with three kinds of Ce-MOFs at only $1.25~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Figure 3f). At a low concentration of $0.13~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ Ce-MOF 3, the scavenging rate could reach $23.2\%$ , while the activity growth reached a plateau phase at $5~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . The $\mathrm{Ce}$ -MOF 1–3 were barely able to scavenge or generate the OH· (Figures S8 and S9, Supporting Information).
Considering the pathological characteristics of DED, the human corneal epithelial cells (HCECs) were utilized to establish an $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -induced oxidative stress model in order to investigate the antioxidant properties of Ce-MOFs. As anticipated, exposure to $400\upmu\mathrm{{m}}\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{2}$ significantly elevated intracellular ROS levels in HCECs (Figure 4a). The presence of the common antioxidant Nacetyl-L-cysteine (NAC) at a concentration of $10~\mathrm{m}\mathbf{u}$ partially attenuated ROS levels in HCECs. Surprisingly, Ce-MOF groups at a concentration of $20\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , particularly Ce-MOF 3, exhibited much lower fluorescent signals for ROS. The level of intracellular ROS was measured using a microplate reader method, and the results demonstrated significant dose-dependent ROS reduction by Ce-MOFs (Figure 4b–d). Incubation with NAC resulted in a $29-37\%$ decrease in ROS production. While Ce-MOF 1 showed a significant reduction of ROS production $48\%$ at $20\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ) compared to the NAC group, Ce-MOF 2 and Ce-MOF 3 exhibited greater efficacy at the same concentration, resulting in further reinforcement of suppressive effects $60.62\%$ and $60.5\%$ reduction respectively). The difference in capacity turned unnoticeable 6 at concentrations exceeding $20\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , which might be attributed to the saturation of ROS scavenging activity. The flow cytometry results revealed that the ${\mathrm{ROS}}^{+}$ rate of cells treated with medium $(71.4\%)$ , Ce-MOF 1 $(30.1\%)$ , Ce-MOF 2 $(21.5\%)$ , and Ce-MOF 3 $(16.7\%)$ groups confirmed the remarkable antioxidant properties of Ce-MOF 3 (Figure 4e). The cerium oxide nanoparticles (CNPs) with particle size ranging from $3{-}4~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S10, Supporting Information) were synthesized and included for comparative analysis. The flow cytometers results indicated that the mean fluorescent intensity of ROS in HCECs were significantly reduced with treatment of $10\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ Ce-MOFs and CNPs. Interestingly, Ce-MOF 3 exhibited more potent antioxidant capacity with significance than CNPs and Ce-MOF 1 at the equal concentration (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Likewise, the Ce-MOF 3 showed the capacity to reduce intracellular $0_{2}^{~\bullet-}$ level (Figure S12, Supporting Information).
## 2.3. In Vitro Cytotoxicity Evaluation of Ce-MOFs Nanozymes
Prior to the anti-DED experiment in vivo, the safe dose of CeMOFs was required to be determined by cytotoxicity tests, including cell counting kit 8 (CCK-8), living/dead staining, and annexin V/PI staining method. As shown in Figure 5a, no obvious toxicity of Ce-MOFs was observed at the concentration within $80~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . However, the cell viability of the Ce-MOFs was slightly $<80\%$ at $160~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , which might be attributed to redox effect of remaining Ce-MOFs on chromogenic reaction of CCK-8 agent. Therefore, it was necessary to combine multiple experiments to verify cytotoxicity. In the live/dead assay, the majority of the HCECs were stained with Calcein AM, indicating their viability as living cells, and only a small percentage were labelled with PI (Figure 5b). The consistent results were measured and validated through employment of an automatic cell counter (Figure 5c). Futhermore, the annexin V/PI method was carried out to determine the cellular apoptosis level of HCECs exposed to $160\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of Ce-MOFs. According to flow cytometry results in Figure 5d, both early and late apoptosis rates of Ce-MOF 1, CeMOF 2 and Ce-MOF 3 decreased in sequence, and the latter two results were close to those of the medium group. Interestingly, compared with Ce-MOF 1 $26.33\%\pm6.80\%$ , JC-1 monomer), the smaller size of Ce-MOF 2 $(7.41\%\pm3.36\%)$ , and Ce-MOF 3 $(4.83\%\pm1.67\%)$ could minimize the disturbance to the mitochondrial membrane potential of HCECs (Figure 5f,g). For safety concerns, the concentration of Ce-MOFs was limited to no more than $80\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . The ultra-small Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes were an appealing choice for anti-DED therapy due to their more powerful catalytic capability, improved ROS suppression, and lower cytotoxicity, taking into account the aforementioned results.
## 2.4. In Vivo Anti-DED Therapy with Ultra-Small Ce-MOF 3 Nanozymes
Inspired by the clinical efficacy of Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes, the mechanism of anti-DED treatment was further investigated by histopathological experiment. The pathological process of DED was characterized by corneal epithelium defects and a reduction in the number of goblet cells. In normal mice, the corneal surface is tightly and regularly arranged by corneal epithelial cells, which maintains the physiological homeostasis of the cornea. After long-term exposure to BAC, the arrangement and morphology of corneal epithelium were disrupted, featured by desquamation, and thinning of corneal epithelium layer (Figure S13c, cornea, Supporting Information). The cornea of DED mice exhibited edema, as evidenced by loosening and thickening of the stromal layer. Excitingly, treatment of Ce-MOF 3 recovered the morphology and structure of corneal epithelium. In Figure 6f, the thickness of corneal epithelial layer in Ce-MOF 3 group $(20\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $31.69\pm4.894~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ; $40\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $35.10\pm3.277~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ; $80\ \upmu\mathrm{g\mL^{-1}}$ , $37.80\pm2.441~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ) was significantly increased compared with PBS group $(21.97\pm2.808~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . The mucus is the vital component bathing the ocular surface and is mainly secreted by goblet cells. The goblet cells are abundantly distributed along the normal conjunctiva, and the loss of goblet cells is closely associated with DED.[25] In BAC modeling mice, goblet cells turned atrophic and scattered on the conjunctiva (Figure S13c, goblet cells,
Supporting Information). However, the pathologic change of goblet cells was ameliorated following treatment with Ce-MOF 3. The number of goblet cells in PBS group $(13.33\pm3.502$ ) and Ce-MOF 3 group $(20~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $26.50\pm6.091$ ; $40~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $40.17\pm9.538$ ; $80\ \upmu\mathrm{g\mL^{-1}}$ , $41.17\pm8.472$ ) further confirmed the markable therapeutic efficacy of Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes (Figure 6g). The results were analogous to those of the Schirmer test in Figrue 6d. It is speculated that protecting effect of Ce-MOF 3 on both corneal epithelium and goblet cells is possibly due to its anti-oxidation and anti-inflammation properties.
To further investigate the anti-DED mechanism of Ce-MOF 3, the ROS and inflammatory cytokines condition of ocular surface tissue were studied. The corneal tissue is normally in a state of equilibrium, which was, however, broken by exogenous stimulus leading to unexpected overgeneration of ROS and elevated expression of interleukin- $1\beta$ $(\mathrm{IL}\cdot1\beta)$ and cytokeratin 10 (K10), especially in corneal epithelial layer. After BAC modeling, the increased level of ROS or $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ in corneal epithelium (Figures S4 and S13d, Supporting Information) was detected, and secondary oxidative injury could cause cellular apoptosis. As anticipated, the excessive ROS signal of corneal epithelium was eliminated by Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes (Figure 6h). The result is in line with thickness of corneal epithelium, indicating suppressing oxidative injury could minimize the damage to structure of corneal epithelial layer. IL- $\cdot1\beta$ is a key proinflammatory cytokine involved in the pathological process of dry eye.[26] The strong signal of $\mathrm{IL}{\cdot}1\beta$ was observed in DED mice only treated with PBS, while residual $\operatorname{IL-1}\beta$ found on the corneal epithelium was markedly reduced with Ce-MOF 3 treatment (Figure 6i; Figure S13d, Supporting Information). In enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) assay, the $\mathrm{IL}{\cdot}1\beta$ level in DED mice $(597.90\pm91.92\mathrm{pg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ was coincidently reduced in $40\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ Ce-MOF 3 group $(386.80\pm86.27\mathrm{\pg\mL^{-1}})$ and $80\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ CeMOF 3 group $(409.50\pm37.59\mathrm{pg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ (Figure 6k). Unignorably, keratinization, a squamous metaplasia, is another pathological change affecting corneal transparency. The expression of K10, a biomarker of keratinization, was present on superficial layer of epithelium in DED mice (Figure S13d, Supporting Information), and formation of keratin was inseparable from the persisting stimulation of ROS and inflammation.[27] As expected, the K10 expression was barely detected with the treatment of Ce-MOF 3, which poses potent inhibition on squamous metaplasia, and it might be attributed to ROS scavenging and inflammation alleviation by Ce-MOF 3 (Figure 6j).
The ocular distribution and residence time of the ultra-small Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes were investigated by tear samples detection and histopathological observation (Figure 7a). The Ce-MOF 3 was labeled by 6-Aminofluorescein (6-AFL) to obtain 6-AFL/CeMOF 3. The fluorescent spectrum results of purified 6-AFL/CeMOF 3 confirmed the success of conjugation (Figure S14, Supporting Information). The retention behavior of Ce-MOF 3 on the ocular surface was determined by detecting the fluorescent signal of 6-AFL in the collected tear samples within one hour. The results showed that the relative fluorescent unit (RFU) of tear in 6-AFL/Ce-MOF 3 group maintained a strong level in the first $30~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 7b). By contrast, the fluorescent signal of tear in the 6-AFL solution group was significantly lower at $10~\mathrm{min}$ and dramatically decreased at $30~\mathrm{min}$ mark, indicating the rapid clearance of 6-AFL by tear turnover (Figure 7b). To visualize the ocular distribution of Ce-MOF 3, the frozen-sectioned samples were photographed by fluorescent microscopy. Similarly, the weak signal on the ocular surface (e.g., cornea and fornical conjunctiva) was observed in the 6-AFL solution group, while the significantly stronger signal was detected in the 6-AFL/CeMOF 3 group (Figure 7c–f). Interestingly, the Ce-MOF 3 was found, not limited to the superficial corneal epithelium, penetrating into the deeper tissue, and detected in the intercellular space between the basal corneal epithelial cells (Figure 7g; Figure S15, Supporting Information). It is well known that the corneal epithelium, being a natural drug barrier, effectively prevents the entry of most hydrophilic molecules from the external environment.[17a] It is speculated that the ultra-small particle size of Ce-MOF 3 might explain the transepithelial phenomenon. The access to the deeper section of tissue, where conventional drug hardly reaches, might enable Ce-MOF 3 to exert antioxidative efficacy more efficiently in an outside-in manner. Moreover, Ce-MOF 3 was capable of infiltrating through the Goblet cells and adjacent tissues (Figure $\lvert\mathrm{g}\rvert$ , which are mainly located on the epithelial layer of fornical conjunctiva for secreting mucins. The antioxidative protection on Goblet cells would suppress the apoptosis, and facilitate the tear film stability and recovery. Aforementioned results of ocular pharmacokinetics revealed the remarkable anti-DED efficacy of Ce-MOF 3 can be partially ascribed to its ocular penetration facilitated by the ultra-small particle size.
## 2.5. Safety Evaluation in Ocular Application with Ce-MOF 3 Nanozymes
To evaluate the therapeutic translation potential of Ce-MOF 3, the ocular acute irritation and long-term response were thoroughly investigated. Figure 8 depicts the safety assessment of the Ce-MOF 3 ocular surface. The concentration of Ce-MOF 3 at $80~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ was chosen for the ocular safety assessment based on the foregoing results. First, considering rabbits were ideal for eye irritation assessments, the ocular acute irritation reaction of rabbits to Ce-MOF 3 was evaluated within $24\mathrm{h}$ utilizing slit lamp microscopy, fluorescein staining, and H&E staining of corneal tissue. Slit lamp microscopy demonstrated no conjunctival congestion, conjunctival edema, corneal opacity, or defect of the corneal epithelium at 1, 6, or $24\mathrm{~h~}$ post-instillation, indicating no evident ocular surface irritation (Figure 8a). Paraffin sectioning and H&E staining were used to evaluate the corneal structure. (Figure 8a H&E). Similarly, the corneal structure of the Ce-MOF 3 group revealed no variation from the PBS group, including corneal thickness, epithelial and endothelial integrity.
Monitoring the side effects of long-term usage of Ce-MOF 3 on ocular anterior and posterior segments was another primary evaluation, in addition to short-term observations. Following a two-week instillation of Ce-MOF 3, no aberrant changes in corneal and retinal structure were seen in H&E staining (Figure 8b–d). Furthermore, there was no significant difference in electroretinography (ERG) data among the PBS and Ce-MOF 3 groups for both a-wave and b-wave, demonstrating that regular use of Ce-MOF 3 did not interfere with retinal electrical activity (Figure 8e–g). These findings indicate that Ce-MOF 3 eye drops have no ocular toxicity and hold potential for promising applications.
## 3. Conclusion
Dry eye disease is a common and lifelong ocular disease, which cause considerable medical and economic burden on human. In this study, three Ce-MOFs of different sizes were developed by adjusting the concentration of raw materials. Compared with CeMOF 1 and Ce-MOF 2, the obtained Ce-MOF 3 exhibited the smallest size, but no difference in XRD patterns and FTIR results. All Ce-MOFs could strongly scavenge the ${\sf O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with SOD and CAT-mimetic activity, respectively in vitro. Notably, Ce-MOF 3 was superior to both Ce-MOF 1 and Ce-MOF 2 properties in some aspects, such as ROS scavenging, intracellular ROS inhibition, and cytotoxicity. Moreover, the ultra-small Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes could efficiently penetrate into ocular tissue and alleviate the DED symptom, with outstanding biocompatibility both in short-term and long-term for clinical translation.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials and Reagents: Cerium ammonium nitrate $((\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{2}\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{6})$ , terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) were purchased from Aladdin (Shanghai, China). BAC and human recombinant insulin were purchased from Sigma (St. Louis, MO, USA). The SOD assay kit and superoxide anion assay kit were purchased from Nanjing Jiancheng Biological Engineering Institute (Nanjing, China). Hydrogen peroxide detection kit, NAC, CCK-8, and JC-1 kit were purchased from Beyotime (Shanghai, China). All reagents were used as purchased without further purification.
Synthesis and Characterization of $C e$ -MOFs Nanozymes: Based on the hydrothermal synthesis method, three kinds of Ce-MOFs nanozymes with variant particle sizes were synthesized by regulating the ratio of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ and $(\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{2}\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{6}$ , named Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3. As for the synthesis of Ce-MOF 1, $0.708\:\mathrm{g}\mapsto\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ was introduced into $24~\mathsf{m L}$ DMF solution under magnetic stirring until completely dissolved. Then $8m L$ aqueous solution containing $2.32\ \mathrm{g}\ (\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{2}\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{6}$ was added. After 10 min stirring, the resulting mixture was transferred into a Teflonlined autoclave and sealed tightly. Finally, the Teflon-lined autoclave was maintained at ${\mathsf{100}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for $\textsf{l h}$ . After cooling to room temperature naturally, the precipitates were collected and dialyzed using dialysis membranes (Yuanye Bio-Technology, 14 000 Da MWCO cut off) for 2 days at room temperature against deionized water. After dialysis, the solution was concentrated for further use. Ce-MOF 2 and Ce-MOF 3 samples were created using similar synthesis procedures, with the exception of varying ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ and $(\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{2}\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{6}$ input quantities: Ce-MOF $2\colon0.354\:\mathrm{g}\mapsto\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ and $\overline{{1.16~\mathrm{g}}}$ $\textsf{g}({\mathsf{N H}}_{4})_{2}{\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{6};$ ; Ce-MOF $3\colon0.177\ \mathsf{g}\ \mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ and $_{0.558\mathrm{~g~}}$ $(\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{2}\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{6}$ .
Other Characterization of $C e$ -MOFs Nanozymes: The morphology and microstructure of Ce-MOFs were observed by SEM (HITACHI S-7400, Japan) and high-resolution TEM (JEM2100F, Japan) at a voltage of $200\mathsf{k V}.$ High-temperature XRD patterns were recorded by the Empyrean X-ray powder Diffractometer (Holland). The functional groups were investigated through FTIR spectra ranging from 400 to $4000c m^{-1}$ . XPS experiments were carried out using a Thermo SCIENTIFIC ESCALAB 250Xi spectrometer. The signal of ESR spectroscopy was measured by a Bruker A300 spectrometer (Bruker, Germany). BET measurements were performed on a surface area and porosimetry analyzer (QUANTACHROME, USA). The TGDSC curves were recorded by a TA Discovery SDT 650 Synchronous thermal analyzer (USA).
Catalyst Properties of $C e$ -MOFs Nanozymes: To evaluate the ROS scavenging ability of the Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 nanozymes in vitro, the ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ scavenging ability of the obtained Ce-MOFs nanozymes was detected. Three kinds of Ce-MOFs were ultrasonicated for 10 min before the test, and different concentrations were prepared in deionized water by gradient dilution method.
$H_{2}O_{2}$ Scavenging Activity of $C e$ -MOFs Nanozymes: The hydrogen peroxide assay kit (S0038, Beyotime) was used to evaluate the ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ scavenging activity of Ce-MOFs. The Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 at different concentrations $(70-640~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1},$ ) were prepared. Then, the equivalent volume of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ $(500\upmu\upnu)$ was added and incubated for 30 min in the dark. After centrifugation at 12 000 rpm for 5 min, the ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ concentration of the collected supernatant was determined by reacting it with an ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ detection solution for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . The absorbance was determined by a microplate reader (Molecular Devices Spectramax M5) at $562~\mathsf{n m}$ , and the ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ scavenging rate was calculated. The thermal stability of CeMOFs was evaluated by comparing the ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ scavenging activity before and after autoclaved sterilization ( $\mathsf{l o3\ k P a}$ , $121^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , $15\mathrm{\min}$ ), and storage stability was assessed under different conditions $(-20,4$ , and $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ over time.
$O_{2}\bullet^{-}$ Scavenging Activity of Ce-MOFs Nanozymes: The $\mathsf{O}_{2}\bullet^{-}$ scavenging activity of Ce-MOFs was evaluated by a superoxide anion assay kit. Briefly, four kinds of reagents were placed in $1.5~\mathsf{m L}$ tubes as the instructions indicated. Then, $50~\upmu\up L$ Ce-MOFs $(0.73{-}80\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m L}^{-1})$ , as the sample group, or deionized water, as control group, were mixed with the above solution for 40 min of incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . After the reaction, the $2m L$ chromogenic agent was added, and the absorbance value at $550\mathsf{n m}$ was measured by a microplate reader. The Ce-MOF 3 was introduced into dimethyl sulfoxide solution containing $2.5\mathsf{m m}\mathsf{K O}_{2}$ , 25 mm BMPO, and $3.5\mathsf{m w}\mathsf{l}8.$ crown-6 for ESR spectroscopy assay.
The equation of the $\mathsf{O}_{2}\bullet^{-}$ scavenging ratio was shown as follows:
$$
\mathsf{O2^{*-}s c a v e n g i n g\ r a t i o\Gamma}\left(\mathcal{T}\right)=\frac{\mathsf{A}_{\mathsf{c o n t r o l}}-\mathsf{A}_{\mathsf{s a m p l e}}}{\mathsf{A}_{\mathsf{c o n t r o l}}}\times100\
$$
wherein, $A_{\mathsf{c o n t r o l}}$ is the absorbance after the reaction with deionized water, and $A_{\mathsf{s a m p l e}}$ is the absorbance with Ce-MOFs.
SOD Activity of $C e$ -MOFs Nanozymes: To verify the SOD-like enzyme activity of Ce-MOF nanozymes, the SOD detection kit was used. The generation of $0_{2}\bullet-$ was obtained by catalyzing xanthine and ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ with xanthine oxidase, which could be decomposed by SOD. The amount of residual $\mathsf{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ was quantified by WST-1 to determine the SOD activity of CeMOFs. The addition of $20\upmu\upiota$ Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 (2.5– $160\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}.$ ), $20~\upmu\upiota$ enzyme working solution, and $200~\upmu\upiota$ substrate solution was carried out in a 96-well plate sequentially. Following 20 min incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , the absorbance value of the solution at $450~\mathsf{n m}$ was measured with a microplate analyzer.
Cell Evaluation of Ce-MOFs Nanozymes: All operations were performed under sterile conditions and cells were kept in a $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ incubator (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) supplied with $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The HCECs were provided by the American Type Culture Collection. HCECs were cultured in DMEM/F12 medium (Gibco, USA) supplemented with $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (Gibco, USA), $5~{\upmu\upxi}~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ insulin (Sigma, USA), and $10~\mathrm{{ng}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ human epidermal growth factor (Sigma, USA).
Cytotoxicity: The CCK-8, Calcein-AM $/\mathsf{P l}$ double staining, Annexin V/PI staining, and JC-1 staining methods were performed to comprehensively assess the cytotoxicity of three kinds of Ce-MOFs. As for CCK-8 method, $5\times10^{3}$ cells were seeded in a 96-well plate and cultivated overnight. The Ce-MOF 1, Ce-MOF 2, and Ce-MOF 3 were introduced to wells at a final concentration ranging from 1.25 to $160\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ for a $24\mathsf{h}$ incubation. After the addition of $70\upmu\upiota$ CCK-8 reagent, the absorbance was determined by a microplate reader at $450~\mathsf{n m}$ . The $5\times10^{4}$ of HCECs were placed in 24-well plates and grew overnight, followed by incubation with three kinds of Ce-MOFs $(40-760~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathsf{m L}^{-1})$ for $24\ h$ . The Calcein-AM and PI reagent (Solarbio, China) were used to determine live and dead cells as the instruction described; then the fluorescent images of HCECs were captured by microscope (DMI8, Leica) and the proportion of viable cells, labeled by Calcein-AM, was determined by Celldrop cell counter (Denovix). The early apoptosis and late apoptosis of HCECs treated with Ce-MOFs were estimated via the annexin $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{P l}$ kit (BD Biosciences). Meanwhile, considering nanozymes could probably pose harm to mitochondria, the mitochondrial membrane potential of HCECs was evaluated by JC-1 assay. The HCECs were cultured in 24-well plates overnight at a density of $5\dot{\times}10^{4}$ cells per well. The HCECs with $24\ h$ treatment of $160\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ Ce-MOFs were digested and collected gently, and the following operation was performed according to the manufacturer’s instructions. Flow Cytometry (C6 Plus, BD Accuri) was used to collect Annexin V/PI and JC-1 results, and data were processed using FlowJo 10 software.
In Vitro Antioxidant Stress Ability of Ce-MOFs Nanozymes: The ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ was applied to establish an oxidative stress model of HCECs for evaluating the antioxidant stress ability of Ce-MOFs. Afterward, the intracellular ROS level was detected by 5-(and-6)-chloromethyl- $^{2^{\prime},7^{\prime}}$ - dichlorodihydrofluorescein diacetate, acetylester $(C M\cdot H_{2},D C F D A$ , Invitrogen). In this study, $5\times10^{3}$ HCECs were seeded into 96-well black/clear bottom plate and cultured at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathsf{h}$ . After that, medium, Ce-MOFs $(2.5{\cdot}80~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1})$ ) and typical antioxidant NAC ( $10\ m\mathbf{M})$ ), were added to each well and incubated for $24\mathsf{h}$ . Subsequently, the supernatants of each well were replaced by $100\upmu\mathsf{L}\mathsf{C M}.\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{C F D A}$ working solution diluted in PBS (Biosharp) for 30 min of incubation. Following the removal of CM${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{D C F D A}}$ solution, the cells were stimulated by ${\mathsf{100}}{\mathsf{\upmu L}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ $(400\upmu\upnu)$ in DMEM/F12 medium without serum for $2h$ . The fluorescent intensity of HCECs was detected at $488~\mathsf{n m}$ (excitation wave) and $525~\mathsf{n m}$ (emission wave) by microplate reader in the form of bottom read, and the ROS inhibition rate was calculated. The ROS fluorescent images of HCECs were taken by microscopy. Meanwhile, the ROS level of cells was detected by Flow Cytometry (Attune Nxt, Thermo) and results were analyzed by FlowJo 10.
Animal Experiments: Male $B A L B/c$ mice (age, 7–8 weeks) and male Japanese white-eared rabbits (weight, $2.0{-}2.5\ \mathsf{k g})$ ) without ocular disease were selected for animal experiments under the protocols approved by the Eye and ENT Hospital of Fudan University Ethics Committee (IACUCDWZX-2021-011). All animals were kept under a $12\textmd{h}$ light/ $12\mathsf{h}$ dark environment and supplied with sufficient food and water.
Anti-DED Therapy by Ultra-Small $C e$ -MOF 3 Nanozymes: Topical $0.2\%$ BAC was applied to establish a dry eye model.[28] Prior to the experiment, healthy $B A L B/c$ mice with no ocular surface abnormalities were selected. The mice were topically instilled with 5 μL $0.2\%$ BAC twice a day (8 AM, 8 PM) for successive 14 days. The corneal epithelial defects were assessed by dropping 2 μL $1\%$ fluorescein sodium solution $(\mathsf{w}/\mathsf{v})$ and then observed with slit lamp microscopy (Kanghua, China) under cobalt blue light on the 7th day of modeling. As previously reported,[24] the cornea was divided into 5 sectors (nasal, temporal, superior, inferior, and central), and scores of each sector were determined as follows: no staining $=0$ , slight punctate staining $=1$ , distinct punctate or slight coalescent staining $=2$ , distinct coalescent or slight patchy staining $=3$ , distinct patchy staining $=4$ . Corneal epithelial defects were scored by one experienced ophthalmologist blind to experimental grouping. The experimental mice with similar scores were divided into four groups and started receiving topical administration of $70\upmu\upiota$ PBS, Ce-MOF 3 (20, 40, and $80\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}.$ ) twice a day. Tear secretion condition was assessed by the Schirmer test as previously reported.[29] On the premise of not touching the eyelid and ocular surface, phenol red thread was inserted in the tear meniscus for $75\ s.$ . The length of the wetted thread was measured and recorded.
Anti-DED Mechanism by Ultra-Small Ce-MOF 3 Nanozymes: During the period of treatment, the clinical score was carried out on days 0, 7, 9, 11, 13. On day 11, the eyeballs of mice were carefully harvested after euthanasia for histopathologic study. One part of the eyeballs was used for paraffin pathological staining, where $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ paraffin sections were stained by hematoxylin-eosin dye (H&E, BaSO, China) and Periodic Acid-Schiff (PAS) dye. Whereafter, the corneal epithelial thickness and number of goblet cells were analyzed. Another part was frozen in optimal cutting temperature compound for fluorescent staining. The tissues were frozen-sectioned $(72\upmu\mathrm{m})$ and fixed in $4\%$ paraformaldehyde for 15 min. The ROS level in the cornea was stained by frozen sections ROS detecting kit (GENMED, USA) according to manufacturer’s instructions. The $1L-7\beta$ (1:200, Abam) and K10 (1:200, Abam) expression of the corneal was assessed by fluorescent staining method. The fluorescence signal of tissue was recorded by fluorescent microscopy (APX 100, Olympus). The mouse IL- $\rceil\beta$ enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay kit (R&D Systems, USA) was used to determine the $1L-7\beta$ expression in cornea.
Ocular Pharmacokinetics of $C e$ -MOF 3 Eye Drops: The carboxy group of Ce-MOF 3 was activated by 1-ethyl-3- (3-dimethylaminopropyl)carbodiimide hydrochloride and Nhydroxysuccinimide overnight. The 6-AFL was introduced and reacted for $4h$ . The obtained final products (6-AFL/Ce-MOF 3) were centrifugated and washed three times for purification. The $2\upmu\upiota$ of 6-AFL/Ce-MOF 3 and 6-AFL solution was instilled on the eyes of mice. At specific time intervals (10, 30, and $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ ), the tear samples were collected by capillary tubes, and the fluorescent signal of tear was measured by microplate reader (ex $488~\mathsf{n m}$ , em $525~\mathsf{n m}$ ). To observe the distribution of Ce-MOF 3, the eyeball and eyelid tissue were collected for frozen-section experiment. The fluorescent images were taken by microscopy (APX 100, Olympus).
Safety Evaluation: On account of excellent anti-DED therapeutic outcome, both short-term and long-term ocular biosafety evaluation of ultrasmall Ce-MOF 3 was required for potential clinical translation. The evaluations can be conducted through slit lamp bio-microscopy, ERG (Diagnosys), and histopathological observation. Firstly, the ocular acute irritation response of Ce-MOF 3 in rabbits was investigated by the modified Draize test. In brief, $50\upmu\up L$ of $80\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ Ce-MOF 3 was dropped into the lower conjunctival sac of the right eye, while the left eye was treated with PBS as a control. The ocular manifestation was recorded by a slit lamp at 1, 6, and $24\ h$ post-instillation, and corneal epithelial integrity was observed by fluorescein staining at $6\mathfrak{h}$ . Ultimately, the H&E images of corneal tissue structure were taken by microscopy (APX 100, Olympus). As for prolonged observation, the right eyes of $B A L B/c$ mice were treated with $80\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\dot{\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ Ce-MOF 3 twice a day for consecutive 14 days, whereas the left eyes were treated with PBS. On day 14, the corneal and retinal structures as well as visual function were estimated via H&E staining and ERG testing. The ERG signal was acquired in accordance with the standard protocol established by the International Society for Clinical Electrophysiology of Vision. The results under Dark-adapted 1.0 ERG were included for analysis.
